---
import { link } from "../../../lib/url";
import SourcesPage from "../../../components/SourcesPage.astro";
import { DS, GENERATED, SURVEY, RBI, BUDGET } from "../../../lib/gdp/data";
import { DERIVED_STATES } from "../../../lib/gdp/states";

const files: [string, string][] = [
  ["gdp.json", "Everything on this site: national accounts by series, year and estimate stage; Budget assumptions; forecasts"],
  ["national_accounts.csv", "Every row MoSPI published, one value per line, with names as printed and the source URL"],
  ["per_person.csv", "GDP, national income, GNI and household spending per person, with the population used, by series and year (latest estimate)"],
  ["states.json", "State accounts by state and year: GSDP, NSDP, income per person, growth, value added by industry"],
  ["state_accounts.csv", "Every state-accounts row MoSPI published, one value per line, as printed, with the source URL"],
  ["validation_report.md", "Every check and every documented exception"],
  ["validation_summary.json", "The result of each group of checks, for programs: checks, failures and allowed exceptions"],
];
const updated = new Date(GENERATED).toLocaleDateString("en-IN", { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric" });
---
<SourcesPage area="gdp" files={files} description="Where every GDP figure on India Data Review comes from, and how it is checked.">
  <p>
    National and state accounts come from the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI), through its official
    data service (<a href="https://esankhyiki.mospi.gov.in" rel="external noopener">eSankhyiki</a>, api.mospi.gov.in).
    Forecasts come from the Economic Survey and the Union Budget (<a href="https://www.indiabudget.gov.in" rel="external noopener">indiabudget.gov.in</a>)
    and the Reserve Bank of India (<a href="https://www.rbi.org.in" rel="external noopener">rbi.org.in</a>). Population,
    for figures per person, comes from the official projections (National Commission on Population) and MoSPI's press
    notes, checked against the RBI's Handbook of Statistics on the Indian Economy. A program
    downloads every file, keeps a copy with its fingerprint (SHA-256), builds the data and checks it. Data last built {updated}.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="checks">
    <li><strong>Accounting identities</strong> in every year, estimate and price basis: industries add up to total GVA; GDP = GVA + net product taxes; net taxes = taxes − subsidies; NDP = GDP − depreciation; national income = GDP + income from abroad; investment and saving by sector add up to the totals.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth rates</strong>: every published annual and quarterly growth rate, for GDP and for each industry, is compared with the levels it should follow from.</li>
    <li><strong>Quarters</strong> add up to the year.</li>
    <li><strong>State accounts</strong>: each state's GSDP and NSDP add up from its industries, taxes and subsidies; industries add up to total value added; every published state growth rate follows from the levels; and the per-person tables agree.</li>
    <li><strong>Population and figures per person</strong>: the population projections are read from the official report and must add up (men plus women) and rise every year; every per-person figure MoSPI or the RBI printed implies the same population within its table, close to the projection for that year, and MoSPI's documents agree with each other; the population used agrees with the RBI's to the lakh; and every printed per-person figure whose total is the same estimate as MoSPI's latest is reproduced to within ₹2 (where MoSPI has revised a total since, there is nothing to compare).</li>
    <li><strong>The long-run back series</strong> (1950-51 to 2011-12) joins the current series in 2011-12.</li>
    <li><strong>Forecasts</strong>: each of the {SURVEY.length} Economic Survey and {RBI.length} RBI forecasts is stored with the sentence it was read from; the program finds that sentence in the downloaded document and checks the numbers in it. The {BUDGET.length} Budget GDP assumptions are read from the Budget at a Glance PDFs and their stated growth is re-computed.</li>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="checks-note">
    <p>
      Some comparisons are listed in the report for review, not checked, so they cannot stop the build. One is the
      states' GSDP added up and compared with India's GDP: the total usually comes to within a few percent of it, but
      the states are compiled separately and keep being revised, so the two are not expected to agree.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="after-checks">
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Which numbers are shown</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Two series.</strong> MoSPI's 2011-12-base series (released January 2015) and its new 2022-23-base series (27 February 2026). Their levels are never mixed. Long-run charts use the 2011-12 base, which MoSPI has carried back to 1950-51.</li>
      <li><strong>Estimate stages.</strong> A year's GDP is first estimated before the year ends and revised up to five times. Charts use the latest estimate unless they say otherwise; the <a href={link("/gdp/revisions/")}>Revisions</a> page shows every stage.</li>
      <li><strong>Growth rates</strong> are shown as MoSPI published them. For the back series (before 2012-13), which MoSPI publishes as levels only, growth is worked out from constant-price GDP and marked "from levels".</li>
      <li><strong>Per person</strong>: MoSPI's latest total for each year divided by India's population at the middle of the year: the official 2019 projections up to {DS.population.projections_until}, then MoSPI's own figures, from its National Accounts Statistics and press notes or, for a year neither covers, from the RBI's reprint of MoSPI's tables. Net national income is GNI less depreciation, worked out as NDP + GNI − GDP. Growth per person is worked out from the constant-price figures. Only the latest estimate of each year has per-person figures. See <a href={link("/gdp/per-person/")}>Per person</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Shares</strong> (of GDP, of GVA) divide two figures from the same year and the same estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>State accounts</strong> are on the 2011-12 base, the only one MoSPI publishes them on, with the latest estimate for each year. Where MoSPI's GSDP table leaves a state out but its value-added table covers it ({DERIVED_STATES.join(", ")}), GSDP is worked out as value added plus taxes minus subsidies on products, and marked.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Problems found in the official data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>MoSPI labels <em>taxes on products</em> and <em>subsidies on products</em> as "₹ lakh", but the values are ₹ crore: taxes minus subsidies equal net product taxes exactly in crore. They are used as crore.</li>
      <li>One national disposable income figure is labelled with the year "1999" instead of a fiscal year. It is left out.</li>
      <li>For 2012-13 and 2013-14, MoSPI publishes two different "Additional Revision" growth rates for {DS.duplicate_rows.length} industry figures. Neither is used.</li>
      <li>Net product taxes for the first advance and provisional estimates of 2014-15 and 2015-16 do not equal GDP minus GVA. GDP and GVA are shown; net taxes for those releases are not.</li>
      <li>Before 2004-05 the back series' industries do not add up to its total GVA (5% apart in 1950-51, 0.3% in 2000-01), as each industry was linked to the new base separately. Sector shares for those years are of the sum of industries.</li>
      <li>Nominal GVA growth in the second advance estimates of 2019-20 is given as 3.3%, but that release's own levels give 7.3% (and its nominal GDP grew 7.4%). It looks mistyped; the site shows the latest estimate for that year instead.</li>
      <li>Some published growth rates cannot be reproduced exactly from today's levels, because they were measured against an earlier estimate of the previous year that MoSPI's data service no longer keeps. They are listed in the report.</li>
      <li>In MoSPI's state accounts, the net state value added table repeats Karnataka's gross value added, and the second table of income per person repeats Karnataka's GSDP per person. A net figure cannot equal the gross one, so both are left out; Karnataka's income per person comes from the first table, which agrees with its GSDP.</li>
      <li>In the Budget 2025-26 PDF, the GDP figures are missing from the text layer; they were read from the page image and confirmed by the 10.1% growth printed beside them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Not here yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rural and urban split.</strong> MoSPI does not estimate GDP by rural and urban area each year (its last such estimate was for 2004-05). Rural and urban consumer prices, unemployment and household spending are on the <a href={link("/jobs-prices/")}>Jobs &amp; prices</a> pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Revision history before 2011-12</strong>, from MoSPI's press releases of 2000-01 to 2010-11 on the earlier bases.</li>
      <li><strong>IMF forecasts</strong> (World Economic Outlook).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  </Fragment>
</SourcesPage>
